\begin_layout Subsection
The Neuron
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Pre-Activation 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The preactivation of neuron 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
a_{i}(\mathbf{x}) & =\sum^{d}_{j=1}w_{ij}x_{j}+w_{i0}\\
 & =\mathbf{w}_{i}'\mathbf{x}_{+1},
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}_{+1}\in\mathbb{R}^{d+1}$
\end_inset

 denotes the argmentet vector 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{x}_{+1}=\begin{bmatrix}1 & x_{1} & \cdots & x_{d}\end{bmatrix}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Activation and output 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Applying a (non-linear) activation function 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{i}:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 yields the output of neuron 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
z_{i}(\mathbf{x})=h_{i}(a_{i}(\mathbf{x}))=h_{i}\left(\sum^{d}_{j=1}w_{ij}x_{j}+w_{i0}\right).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
That is each neuron 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 has its own weights (and bias) 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{w}\in\mathbb{R}^{d+1}$
\end_inset

 and activation function 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 The latter is usually the same for all neurons except the output-neurons (in the top layer).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Activation functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the network structure to have any reason to exist the activation functions 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{i}$
\end_inset

 must be non-linear.
 If not the neural network 
\begin_inset Formula $f:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{K}$
\end_inset

 will simply be a linear function of the inputs aswell.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To see this note that each neuron in the inputlayer would output the following functional form
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
h\left(\sum^{d}_{j=1}w_{ij}x_{j}+w_{i0}\right) & =\sum^{d}_{j=1}\underbrace{aw_{ij}}_{\tilde{w}_{ij}}x_{j}+\underbrace{aw_{i0}+b}_{\tilde{w}_{i0}}\\
 & =\sum^{d}_{j=1}\tilde{w}_{ij}x_{j}+\tilde{w}_{i0},
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and since each pre-activation is simply a affine linear function of the previous affine linear functions all later neurons will output something of the form:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
h\left(a\left(\begin{bmatrix}z_{1} & z_{2} & \cdots & z_{K}\end{bmatrix}\right)\right) & =h\left(\sum^{K}_{k=1}w_{k}z_{k}+w_{0}\right)\\
 & =\sum^{K}_{k=1}\hat{w}_{k}z_{k}+\hat{w}_{0}\\
 & =\sum^{K}_{k=1}\hat{w}_{k}\left(\sum^{d}_{j=1}\tilde{w}_{kj}x_{j}+\tilde{w}_{j0}\right)+\hat{w}_{0}\\
 & =\sum^{K}_{k=1}\hat{w}_{k}\sum^{d}_{j=1}\tilde{w}_{kj}x_{j}+\tilde{w}_{j0}\\
\\
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

